      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. Their problem was that after the taxonomy change on 2026-02-20, Event Nexus search returned many low-relevance hits and duplicates, increasing manual triage time to about 6 hours per day [art_8b0063fbb3cb].\n\nTo get BlueHarbor comfortable with renewal, the proposed proof plan included:\n\n- Updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer to map new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches.\n- Timeline and steps:\n  1) By 2026-03-19, BlueHarbor to provide schema registry export and 14 days of query logs.\n  2) Starting 2026-03-22, run an A/B test in staging for 5 business days tuning plus 2 business days validation (7-10 business days total).\n  3) Validation criteria: top-5 correct hit rate \u2265 80% for prioritized queries, no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n  4) If successful, a 1-day production rollout followed by 72 hours monitoring.\n- A 90-day professional services engagement at $48k fixed price to implement the tuning, mapping layer, and run the proof, with a 30% discount if BlueHarbor commits to Orchestrator seat expansion concurrently.\n- A conditional credit clause: if the proof does not meet the agreed top-5 accuracy uplift within 10 business days, BlueHarbor receives a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against the renewal for the upcoming year.\n- Compatibility tests to ensure no breakage of existing EN-RULES-ENGINE automations.\n- Clear escalation path including potential reindexing (~15 TB data, 48 hours compute, ~$25k cost) if mapping is insufficient [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThis proof plan was designed to demonstrate measurable improvement quickly to satisfy BlueHarbor's executive mandate to reduce manual triage by 40% and to secure renewal confidence.",
